4. Swipe gestures — Attacks and special moves

This is the most important design requirement.

Every available combat move must have an intuitive gesture inspired by its visible animation, travel direction, and gameplay purpose.

Do not simply map Attack 1 to swipe right, Attack 2 to swipe left, and so forth.

That would violate Alva’s controller comfort philosophy.

The controller comfort rules are the reference

The existing controller system prioritizes:

* One natural action per move.
* Common attacks being easy to perform.
* Visible direction over internal attack numbering.
* Upward movement corresponding to upward input.
* Downward movement corresponding to downward input.
* Projectiles corresponding to outward movement.
* Dangerous plunges requiring deliberate action.
* Related ground and air moves sharing a familiar control language.

Slide Controls must embody these same principles.

Swipe directions

Support recognition of:

* Left
* Right
* Up
* Down
* Up-left
* Up-right
* Down-left
* Down-right

The gesture vocabulary may also support curved single-finger swipes for clearly circular or surrounding attacks where a straight direction is not meaningful.

All gestures must remain physically comfortable on a typical phone.

Avoid complicated drawings, multi-stroke symbols, arbitrary combinations, or memorization-heavy patterns.

Facing-relative interpretation

Use the fighter’s facing direction.

For example:

* A forward swipe means the direction the fighter currently faces.
* If facing right, forward means right.
* If facing left, forward means left.
* An up-forward swipe mirrors with facing.
* An up-back swipe mirrors as well.

Swipes that explicitly move something downward should remain downward regardless of facing.

Use visible motion, not an attack’s launch direction, to choose a gesture.

Resolve facing deterministically when the gesture commits. Avoid mapping a single swipe to different attacks because the fighter turned halfway through its motion.

Fighter-specific gesture profiles

Create a built-in gesture definition for every fighter.

Each definition must identify:

* Ground or air
* Existing action codename
* Actual ability
* Gesture shape and direction
* Facing-relative behavior
* Why the gesture matches the move’s visuals
* Whether the move is dangerous or commits the fighter
* Required move availability
* Possible collisions with other gestures

Gesture definitions must be part of the character data system or an appropriate shared registry.

Do not hardcode behavior based on fighter IDs in the gesture recognition engine.

Example design principles

Straight punches and basic attacks: An easy, short forward swipe.

Rising uppercuts, rising kicks, or upward movement: An upward or up-forward swipe.

Downward kicks and slams: Downward or down-forward swipes.

Ranged attacks: A deliberate swipe following the projectile’s visible direction.

Pulling attacks: Consider a swipe toward the fighter when that accurately represents the pulling effect.

Area attacks: Consider a comfortable, short curved gesture if the move visually expands or circulates around the fighter.

Summons: The swipe should reflect where the summoned object appears or moves.

Healing and self-targeted abilities: Use an intuitive gesture directed toward the player’s fighter rather than an arbitrary direction.

Dangerous plunging attacks: Require a deliberate downward gesture, resistant to accidental activation during ordinary movement.

These are design principles, not permission to assign multiple moves to the same input.

Gesture ambiguity is unacceptable

The five current fighters can have several attacks with similar visual directions.

Therefore a simple four-direction classifier is insufficient.

Construct and review a complete move-to-gesture matrix for all current fighters.

Where multiple moves naturally share a direction, evaluate a secondary visible distinction such as:

* Short versus extended motion
* Straight versus curved motion
* Directional diagonal versus horizontal travel
* A deliberate sweeping gesture for a significant Special

Only use distinctions that remain intuitive and consistently recognizable.

Do not distinguish unrelated moves through invisible timing tricks.

Avoid requiring users to count milliseconds or memorize arbitrary swipe lengths.

If a proposed mapping is ambiguous, redesign it before implementation.

Every eligible attack must remain accessible. Do not omit an ability because it is difficult to map.

